select m.id, 1, $L$Designing the Right QA Team$L$, $L$18 minute read$L$, $L$Most QA teams are inherited, not designed — an org chart that grew by accident and now feels permanent. This lesson treats team shape as a design decision driven by organisational risk and context, not by copying whichever model the last conference talk praised. You will learn to reason from risk to structure, and to defend that structure to executives.$L$, array[$L$Derive a QA team shape from a specific organisation's risk profile rather than a generic template$L$, $L$Compare centralised, embedded and hybrid models against concrete trade-offs$L$, $L$Justify a chosen structure to a time-poor executive in the language of risk and delivery$L$], $L$## Start from risk, not from an org chart
The single most common mistake in QA team design is starting with a structure ("we should embed testers in squads" / "we need a centre of excellence") and reverse-engineering a justification. Senior leaders do the opposite: they start from the organisation's **risk profile** and let structure fall out of it.

Ask, concretely: where would a defect hurt most? For Northstar, a payments defect risks money and trust; an accessibility defect risks a specific legal and reputational exposure on the consumer web app; an API regression risks the B2B contracts that the business wants to sell against SLAs. Those risks are not equal, and a team designed as if they were is a team that spends its scarcest people in the wrong place.

## What "the right team" actually optimises for
A QA team is a mechanism for **converting a fixed budget of skilled attention into risk reduction and delivery confidence**. Every design choice — centralised vs embedded, generalist vs specialist, in-house vs contracted — trades some qualities away to buy others:
- **Proximity to the code** (embedded testers catch problems earlier) vs **consistency of standards** (a central team keeps quality bars comparable across squads).
- **Depth** (specialists go deep on performance, security, accessibility) vs **flexibility** (generalists flex to wherever the risk moves this quarter).
- **Speed now** (contract in a capability) vs **capability retained** (grow it in-house so it compounds).

There is no universally correct answer. There is a correct answer *for this organisation, this quarter, at this risk profile and budget* — and it changes.

## The three structural archetypes
- **Centralised QA** — a single team that services squads. Strengths: consistent standards, easy to see the whole quality picture, career home for testers, easier to protect specialist skills. Weaknesses: pulled in late, becomes a bottleneck, "throw it over the wall" dynamics, QA owns quality instead of the whole team owning it. This is Northstar today, and it shows: QA is pulled in once a story is code-complete, then blamed for the five-day regression that lateness makes necessary.
- **Embedded QA** — testers sit inside squads and share their goals. Strengths: early involvement, shared ownership, faster feedback, context-rich testing. Weaknesses: inconsistent standards between squads, professional isolation (one tester per squad has no peers), specialist skills get diluted, and a single embedded tester is easy to quietly deprioritise.
- **Hybrid / matrixed** — testers are embedded in squads for daily work but belong to a QA function (a guild, chapter or community of practice) for standards, career and specialist support. Most scaling organisations converge here because it buys early involvement *and* consistency — at the cost of the classic matrix tension (two masters, competing priorities).

## Where specialists actually belong
Specialist capability — performance, security, accessibility — rarely justifies one embedded specialist per squad; the demand is spiky and the talent is scarce. The usual senior pattern is a **thin central specialist capability that squads pull on**, combined with **raising baseline awareness across generalists** so routine cases are handled in-squad and only genuinely hard problems escalate. We will return to this repeatedly: designing capability is not the same as hiring one of everything.

## How to explain the choice upward
Priya (VP Eng) is data-driven and time-poor; she will not read a treatise on Spotify-model chapters. Frame the design as: "Here is our risk profile. Here is where defects currently escape and what they cost. Here is the structure that puts our scarce skilled attention against the biggest risks, and here is what we are deliberately choosing *not* to cover and why." A design that names its own gaps is far more credible than one that pretends to cover everything.

## What can go wrong
- **Cargo-culting a model** because a famous company uses it, ignoring that their risk profile and scale are nothing like yours.
- **Reorganising structure to fix a behaviour problem** (late involvement is often a process and relationship problem, not a box-diagram problem — moving the boxes changes nothing).
- **Designing for the org you wish you had** rather than the budget and people you actually have.

## How you would know it is working
Not "testers are busy". Better signals: defects are caught earlier (cheaper), specialist risks are covered without a specialist idling, squads stop treating QA as a gate, and the escaped-defect trend bends down. If a reorganisation moves none of those, it was decoration.$L$, $L$You are Northstar's new Head of QA. The instinctive move is to embed one of your six testers into each of the four squads plus keep two central — it looks tidy. Reason from risk instead. Payments carries money-and-trust risk and a sceptical lead (Marcus); the API underpins B2B SLAs the business wants to sell; the consumer web app carries accessibility exposure; Mobile is growing fast. Six people cannot be one-per-squad and also cover performance, security and accessibility, and also maintain automation. So you design deliberately: embed your two strongest exploratory testers where risk and lateness bite hardest (Payments and API), keep a small central function that owns standards, the skills matrix and the flaky automation problem, and treat performance/security/accessibility as thin capabilities you build awareness for now and buy or borrow when a specific risk spikes. You present this to Priya as "scarce attention aimed at money, contracts and legal exposure first" — and you explicitly name Mobile as lighter-touch this quarter, a conscious risk you will revisit. That last sentence is what makes it a design and not a guess.$L$, $L$- Choosing a structure first (embedded / CoE / guild) and justifying it afterwards, instead of deriving it from risk
- Assuming a bigger or more specialised team is a better team — capability is about fit to risk, not headcount
- Treating late QA involvement as an org-chart problem when it is usually a process and relationship problem that a reorg will not touch
- Copying a well-known company's model without matching their scale, risk profile or funding reality$L$, $L$Before you propose any structure, write one page: the organisation's top five quality risks, ranked, with the rough cost of each going wrong. Every subsequent design argument references that page. It converts "I think we should embed testers" into "here is the risk this structure is built to reduce" — which is the only version an executive will fund.$L$, $L$Take Northstar's four squads (Web, Mobile, Platform/API, Payments) plus its six QA engineers. Draft a one-page target operating model: who sits where, what the central function owns, and — critically — which risks you are consciously choosing to cover lightly this quarter and why. Keep it to one page; the discipline is in the omissions.$L$, $L$Think about the QA structure you work in today. Was it designed from the organisation's risk profile, or did it accrete? If you had to defend it to a sceptical VP in two minutes, could you — and if not, what would you change?$L$, $L$A QA leader proposes a full "one embedded tester per squad" model for a six-person team covering four squads, arguing it matches how a famous tech company is organised. What is the strongest objection a senior peer would raise? (Answer: the model is being copied from another organisation rather than derived from Northstar's own risk profile and budget — six people spread one-per-squad cannot also cover specialist risks like performance, security and accessibility, so the design ignores both context and scarcity.)$L$, array[$L$I can derive a QA team shape from a named organisation's risk profile$L$, $L$I can state the concrete trade-offs of centralised, embedded and hybrid models$L$, $L$I can defend a structure to an executive in the language of risk and delivery$L$]
