select m.id, 2, $L$Skills vs Roles$L$, $L$17 minute read$L$, $L$"We need to hire an SDET" is a statement about a role. "We need someone who can stabilise our flaky pipeline and coach developers to write their own tests" is a statement about skills. Confusing the two is how QA teams end up with the wrong people in tidy-looking boxes. This lesson separates roles from skills and shows why senior leaders design, hire and develop around capability.$L$, array[$L$Distinguish a role (a box on a chart) from a skill (a capability that reduces a specific risk)$L$, $L$Map the common QA specialisms to the risks they actually address, and judge when a team does not need one$L$, $L$Explain T-shaped capability and use it to design flexible teams rather than rigid role silos$L$], $L$## A role is a container; a skill is a capability
A **role** is a job title and a scope of responsibility — "Automation Engineer", "SDET", "Performance Tester". A **skill** is a specific capability that reduces a specific risk — "can design a reliable, fast E2E suite", "can profile a service under load and interpret the results", "can run an accessibility audit against WCAG". Roles are how HR, budgets and org charts are structured. Skills are how risk actually gets reduced. Senior leaders think in skills and only package them into roles when they must (to hire, to pay, to promote).

Why it matters: two people with the identical title "QA Engineer" can have almost no overlapping skills, and two people with very different titles can be closer in capability than either would admit. If you plan around titles you will mis-staff; if you plan around skills you will see the real coverage.

## Northstar makes the distinction vivid
Dan's *role* is "Automation Engineer". His actual *skills* and the *risk* the org needs covered have drifted apart: he is maintaining a 1,800-test, six-hour, 25%-flaky Selenium suite alone, which is mostly a test-infrastructure and reliability problem, not the "write more UI tests" problem his title implies. Sofia's *role* is "Senior QA Engineer", but the skills the team leans on are exploratory testing *and* informal leadership and mentoring — leadership she is doing unpaid and unrecognised. If you only read the titles, you would misdiagnose both.

## The common specialisms — and the risk each addresses
Think of these as skills a team may need *some* of, not roles every team must fill:
- **Exploratory testing** — finding the defects that scripted checks and automation never will; addresses the risk of "green pipeline, unhappy users". Almost every team needs strong exploratory skill; it is the most under-valued and least automatable.
- **Test automation (UI/E2E)** — fast, repeatable regression feedback; addresses the risk of slow, expensive manual regression. High value *when the suite is reliable* — an unreliable suite (Northstar's) actively destroys trust and is worse than none.
- **API / service-level test automation** — testing below the UI, where it is cheaper and more stable; addresses regression risk at the integration seams and underpins SLA confidence.
- **SDET / test infrastructure** — building the frameworks, CI integration, test data and environments that make everyone else's testing possible; addresses the risk that testing does not scale with the codebase.
- **Performance testing** — load, stress, soak; addresses the risk of the system falling over under real traffic or breaching an SLA.
- **Accessibility testing** — WCAG conformance and assistive-technology testing; addresses legal, reputational and genuine-inclusion risk on public-facing products.
- **Security-aware testing** — not replacing a security team, but testers who think adversarially and catch the common classes; addresses the risk of shipping obvious vulnerabilities.
- **Domain knowledge** — deep understanding of the business (payments, fintech regulation); addresses the risk of testing the software correctly but missing what actually matters to the business and its users.

## When a team does NOT need a specialist
This is the teaching point the whole module hangs on. A team does **not** need a dedicated specialist when:
- the risk that specialism addresses is **low or rare** for your product (a purely internal tool rarely needs a dedicated accessibility specialist, though it may still need baseline awareness);
- the demand is **too spiky to justify a full-time role** (most orgs need performance testing in bursts, not continuously — buy or borrow it);
- a **generalist with raised awareness** can handle the routine cases and escalate only the genuinely hard ones.
Hiring a specialist you cannot keep busy is not thoroughness; it is a mis-allocation of a scarce budget that could have reduced a bigger risk elsewhere.

## T-shaped capability
A **T-shaped** tester has one area of genuine depth (the vertical) plus enough breadth to contribute across others (the horizontal). A team of T-shaped people is flexible: work is not blocked because "the only automation person is on leave", and specialists still exist where depth genuinely matters. The opposite — narrow "I-shaped" specialists in rigid silos — looks efficient on paper and creates single points of failure and hand-off queues in practice. Designing for T-shapes is how you get both depth and resilience out of a small team.

## How to explain it to stakeholders
When Marcus (the sceptical payments lead) says "good developers don't need a QA team", do not argue the role. Argue the skills and the risk: "You're right that testing isn't a role your developers can outsource responsibility for. What we bring is exploratory depth on the payment edge cases your unit tests won't reach, and someone who owns the reliability of the regression signal you depend on to ship. That's a capability question, not a headcount grab." Skills-and-risk language survives a sceptic; title-and-headcount language does not.

## What can go wrong
- **Title inflation** — calling everyone an SDET because it aids recruitment, then discovering none of them can build test infrastructure.
- **Buying a role to fix a skill gap** you could have developed — hiring an automation engineer when the real need was coaching your existing testers.
- **Silo-ing skills into single people** — the fastest route to a bus factor of one (Dan and the Selenium suite).$L$, $L$Northstar's CTO asks you to "hire two SDETs to fix our automation". Reframe from role to skill before you spend the budget. Diagnose the actual risk: the 1,800-test Selenium suite is slow, 25% flaky, distrusted, and owned by one burning-out person — that is a reliability, ownership and test-design problem, not a "not enough automation engineers" problem. The skills genuinely needed are: test-infrastructure and CI expertise to make the suite fast and stable, the ability to push coverage down to the API level where it is cheaper, and — crucially — the ability to coach developers into owning tests so QA is not the sole owner. One skilled SDET-style hire plus developing Dan and pairing him with the squads may beat two more automation engineers bolted onto a broken suite. You take that back to the CTO as "here are the three skills the automation problem actually needs, and here is the cheapest way to acquire them" — which is a very different, and far more fundable, conversation than "two more headcount".$L$, $L$- Planning capability around job titles instead of the skills that reduce specific risks
- Assuming every professional team needs one of every specialism — many risks are better covered by raised generalist awareness plus occasional buy/borrow
- Hiring a role to solve a problem that was really a development or process gap in the existing team
- Concentrating a critical skill in a single person and calling it a "specialist" rather than what it is — a single point of failure$L$, $L$Whenever someone hands you a role to fill ("we need an SDET"), quietly translate it into the skills-and-risk form before acting: which specific risk is unaddressed, which skills would address it, and is a new hire genuinely the cheapest way to acquire those skills versus developing, borrowing or buying them? Half the time the honest answer changes what you do.$L$, $L$List every named skill this lesson describes. For each, write one sentence stating the risk it reduces, then mark whether Northstar needs it as (a) a dedicated role, (b) a skill developed across generalists, or (c) something to buy or borrow when a specific risk spikes. Be prepared to defend every "dedicated role" against a cost-conscious VP.$L$, $L$Look at your own team's job titles. For each person, how far has their actual skill set drifted from what the title implies — and is the gap a problem, an opportunity, or simply reality that the title has never caught up with?$L$, $L$A CTO asks a QA leader to "hire a performance tester" because a load-related incident just happened. The leader instead proposes training two existing testers in load-test basics and contracting a specialist for two weeks a quarter. What principle is the leader applying? (Answer: designing around skills and risk rather than roles — performance-testing demand is spiky and does not justify a full-time role, so raising generalist capability plus buying occasional specialist depth reduces the risk more cost-effectively than a permanent hire.)$L$, array[$L$I can clearly distinguish a role from a skill and explain why the distinction changes staffing decisions$L$, $L$I can map QA specialisms to the risks they address and judge when a team does not need a dedicated one$L$, $L$I can use T-shaped thinking to argue for flexible capability over rigid silos$L$]
