insert into public.lessons (module_id, lesson_number, title, estimated_time, lesson_overview, learning_objectives, lesson_notes, worked_example, common_mistakes, real_world_tip, exercise, reflection_question, knowledge_check, completion_checklist)
select m.id, 5, $L$Developing Testers$L$, $L$19 minute read$L$, $L$Gap analysis tells you which capabilities to build in-house; this lesson is about how people actually grow. Most development happens on the job, not in a classroom — so a QA leader's real levers are the work they assign, the pairing they arrange and the coaching they give. You will build individual development plans for Northstar's testers, including the two hardest cases: a wary informal lead and a burning-out specialist.$L$, array[$L$Apply an experience-weighted model of development (roughly 70% work, 20% others, 10% formal) to design realistic growth, not just training courses$L$, $L$Write an individual development plan that ties a person's growth to a team capability gap and to their own motivation$L$, $L$Handle the two hardest development cases — a change-wary senior and a burning-out specialist — with concrete, humane moves$L$], $L$## Development is mostly the work, not the course
The instinct when someone needs to grow is to book a training course. Courses have their place, but the large majority of durable skill growth comes from **doing challenging work with support and feedback**, a smaller portion from **learning through others** (pairing, mentoring, coaching, observing), and only a small portion from **formal training** (the widely-cited rough split is 70/20/10). The practical consequence for a leader is that your most powerful development levers are not the training budget — they are the **assignments you hand out** and the **pairings you arrange**. If you develop people only by sending them on courses, you are using the weakest lever and wondering why nothing sticks.

## The leader's development toolkit
- **Stretch assignments:** give someone work just beyond their current level, with a safety net. Chloe (level 2 test design, curious about automation) paired onto stabilising part of Dan's suite is a stretch assignment that closes a team gap *and* grows her.
- **Pairing and shadowing:** the fastest transfer of tacit skill. Pairing a strong exploratory tester with Aisha (the ramping hire) beats any onboarding document.
- **Coaching:** asking questions that help someone reach their own answer, rather than giving them yours. It develops judgement, not just technique, and it scales — a coached tester solves the next problem without you.
- **Communities of practice / guild:** a place for testers across squads to share techniques and standards; this is also how you defeat the isolation an embedded model creates.
- **Formal training / certification:** genuinely useful to establish shared vocabulary and fundamentals quickly (an ISTQB path, a specific tool course) — the 10%, deployed where a knowledge base needs bootstrapping, not as the whole strategy.

## Individual development plans that are not theatre
A useful **individual development plan (IDP)** connects three things: a **team capability gap** the person could help close, the **individual's own motivation and direction**, and a **concrete set of experiences** (not just courses) to get there, with a check-in cadence. The intersection matters: development aimed at a gap the person has no interest in stalls; development aimed at the person's interest but no team gap is a hobby the org is funding. The art is finding work that serves both. Keep IDPs short, specific and revisited — a plan reviewed once a year is a document, not development.

## Northstar's two hard cases
**Sofia — the wary informal lead.** She is an excellent exploratory tester effectively running the team, but she is wary after last year's failed "automate everything" push and has no title or development to match her real role. Her development is not a testing skill — it is deliberate growth into the leadership she is already doing, with recognition. The wariness is rational and must be respected, not steamrolled: co-design her growth, give her a real say in the automation strategy this time (so it is not something done *to* her again), and back her judgement publicly. Rebuilding trust is the prerequisite; skills come after.

**Dan — the burning-out specialist.** He is keen but isolated, owns the flaky suite alone, and is quietly burning out. Piling more automation onto him is not development, it is acceleration toward resignation — and he is your bus factor. His development and the team's key-person risk are the *same problem solved the same way*: pair someone with him (Chloe), which simultaneously relieves his load, transfers his knowledge, and lets him grow *up* — from lone maintainer toward technical leadership and test-infrastructure design, which is a bigger and more sustainable version of his skill. Development and risk mitigation are the same move here.

## How you would explain the development spend to a VP
Priya will not fund "growth" in the abstract. Tie every development action to a gap on the analysis and to retention: "Developing Chloe toward automation closes our worst key-person risk *and* gives her a progression path, which is cheaper than the hire we would otherwise need and cheaper than losing Dan. Here is the cost and the capability it buys." Development framed as risk reduction and retention gets funded; development framed as being nice to staff does not.

## What can go wrong
- **Development-as-courses:** spending the budget on training that does not transfer because it is not reinforced by the actual work.
- **Growing people away from team needs:** funding someone into a skill the team does not need, or ignoring a person's motivation and watching the development stall.
- **Confusing "busy" with "developing":** Dan is extremely busy and not developing at all; overload is the enemy of growth.
- **Steamrolling a wary senior:** treating Sofia's caution as an obstacle rather than earned information will confirm her fear and lose her.

## How you would know it is working
Matrix scores actually move at the next re-assessment; the person can now do unaided what previously needed help; a key-person risk has a genuine second holder; and — the retention signal — the people you are developing are visibly more engaged and are not the ones handing in notice.$L$, $L$Write Dan's IDP as a senior leader would. Do not start with a training course. Start from the intersection: the team's top key-person risk is that Dan alone holds automation and CI, and Dan's own trajectory should be *up and out of firefighting*, not more of the same. So the plan's headline experience is a **pairing arrangement** — Chloe joins him to co-own the suite over two quarters — which relieves his load (addressing burnout and retention), transfers his tacit knowledge (closing the bus factor), and repositions his own work from lone maintainer toward designing a reliable, faster architecture and coaching others (growing him toward level 4 / technical leadership). Alongside it: a small formal element (a specific CI or test-architecture course, the 10%) to give vocabulary to the redesign, and a coaching cadence with you focused on the leadership shift, not the tooling. Every element ties to a gap on the analysis and to Dan's own motivation, and the whole thing doubles as the closure action for the org's most urgent capability risk. That is what makes it fundable and humane at once — and it is a far better answer than "send Dan on an advanced Selenium course", which would develop nothing and change no risk.$L$, $L$- Defaulting to formal training (the 10%) and neglecting stretch assignments and pairing (the 90% that actually builds durable skill)
- Writing IDPs that serve a team gap but ignore the individual's motivation, so the development stalls
- Overloading a specialist and mistaking their busyness for development — overload prevents growth
- Treating a change-wary senior's caution as resistance to overcome rather than earned information to respect and design around$L$, $L$Before booking any training, ask "what real piece of work, with what support, would build this skill better than a course?" Nine times out of ten a stretch assignment plus a pairing beats the course — and it delivers actual output for the team while the person grows, instead of taking them offline for two days to learn something they will forget without reinforcement.$L$, $L$Write short individual development plans (half a page each) for three of Northstar's testers, including Sofia and Dan. Each plan must name: the team capability gap it helps close, the individual's likely motivation, the concrete experiences (weighted toward on-the-job and pairing, not just courses), and a check-in cadence. For Sofia, show explicitly how you rebuild trust before piling on new expectations.$L$, $L$Recall the best professional development you ever experienced. How much of it was a formal course, and how much was challenging work with someone supporting you? What does your honest answer imply about how you should develop your own team?$L$, $L$A QA leader wants to both relieve a burning-out lone automation engineer and close the bus factor his isolation creates. Why is pairing a second engineer with him a single move that addresses both, and what would sending him on an advanced automation course instead fail to achieve? (Answer: pairing simultaneously transfers his tacit knowledge to a second person (closing the bus factor), shares his workload (relieving burnout and aiding retention) and lets him grow toward architecture and coaching — whereas a course develops no second person, closes no key-person risk and does nothing about the overload, so it addresses neither underlying problem.)$L$, array[$L$I can design development that is weighted toward on-the-job experience and pairing rather than formal training$L$, $L$I can write an IDP that intersects a team capability gap with the individual's own motivation$L$, $L$I can handle a change-wary senior and a burning-out specialist with concrete, humane, risk-aware moves$L$]
