select m.id, 6, $L$Creating Career Progression$L$, $L$20 minute read$L$, $L$Northstar has no QA career framework — no levels, no ladder, no way for a tester to see a future beyond their current desk. That is a retention risk, a fairness problem and a recruitment weakness all at once. This closing lesson builds a progression framework: competency-based levels, a dual technical/management ladder so good testers are not forced into management to advance, and the honest conversations that make a ladder real rather than decorative. It also assembles your full portfolio deliverable.$L$, array[$L$Design a competency-based QA progression framework with a dual technical/management ladder appropriate to a mid-sized organisation$L$, $L$Connect the progression framework back to the skills matrix and development plans so it is an operating instrument, not an HR poster$L$, $L$Make the honest structural trade-offs — level inflation, limited headroom, "up or out" — explicit and defensible to executives$L$], $L$## Why progression is a leadership problem, not an HR formality
Northstar's business objectives include controlling cost and reducing churn — and it has "no skills matrix, no progression ladder" for QA. Those facts are connected. Without a visible path, your best people leave for one at another company (a retention cost far exceeding any ladder's design effort), pay and promotion decisions are made ad hoc and feel unfair, and you cannot recruit senior talent who ask, reasonably, "what's the growth path here?" A progression framework is how a leader turns development from a series of one-off favours into a system people can trust.

## Competency-based levels, anchored in the matrix
The cleanest progression frameworks describe each level in terms of **demonstrated competencies and the scope of impact**, not years served or a list of tools. Crucially, use the *same competency language* as your skills matrix (Lesson 3), so the matrix becomes the evidence for a promotion and the framework becomes the target the matrix is assessed against. A typical QA ladder spans roughly: Junior (works on defined tasks with support) → QA Engineer (independent on their own work) → Senior QA (owns quality for an area, sets local standards, mentors) → Lead / Principal (defines approach across teams, org-level influence). Each level states what a person can do and the scope over which they do it — impact widening from "my tasks" to "my squad" to "the whole function".

## The dual ladder — the single most important design choice
The classic mistake is having only one ladder, so the only way to advance (and to earn more) is to become a manager. This quietly punishes your best individual contributors: it either caps their growth or pushes brilliant testers into management they are bad at and unhappy in, losing you a great tester *and* gaining a poor manager. A **dual ladder** splits at senior level into two parallel, equally-paid tracks:
- a **technical/IC track** (Senior → Principal / Staff QA — deep technical leadership, architecture of testing, org-wide technical influence), and
- a **management track** (Senior → QA Manager / Head of QA — leading people, budgets and strategy).
Both must carry genuinely comparable pay and status, or the "dual" ladder is a fiction and everyone still chases the management rung. For Northstar this directly serves two named people: Dan can grow on the technical track toward test-architecture leadership without being forced to manage, and Sofia — already leading — can choose the management track deliberately rather than by default.

## Making a ladder real, not decorative
A framework is only as good as the conversations around it:
- **Promotion means sustained demonstration, not a single good sprint** — the matrix evidence should show the person already operating at the next level, so promotion recognises a reality rather than gambling on potential.
- **Be honest about headroom.** A 55-person company has limited senior slots; pretending otherwise breeds resentment when the ladder stalls. Name it, and use lateral growth (breadth, specialisms, scope) where vertical slots are scarce.
- **Resist level inflation.** Handing out "Senior" titles to retain people cheaply devalues the level for everyone and stores up a pay and credibility problem. A title that does not mean the competencies is worse than no title.
- **Separate the progression conversation from the pay-review conversation** where you can, so growth discussions are not distorted by immediate salary anxiety.

## How to sell it to executives
Frame it to Priya and the CTO in their terms — cost and churn: "A defined ladder reduces regretted attrition, lets us recruit senior QA against a credible path, and makes our promotion and pay decisions defensible and consistent. It uses the competency language we already assess against, so it adds process weight but little new overhead." Progression framed as a retention-and-fairness mechanism with low marginal cost gets approved; progression framed as "our testers want promotions" does not.

## What can go wrong
- **A ladder that is a poster, not a practice** — levels exist on a wiki but promotions still happen by favouritism and volume of hours.
- **A fake dual ladder** — the IC track exists on paper but caps below the management track on pay, so it fools no one.
- **Level inflation under retention pressure**, hollowing out what each level means.
- **Ignoring headroom reality**, promising vertical growth a small org cannot deliver, and losing trust when the ladder jams.

## How you would know it is working
Regretted attrition falls; promotions are predictable and explainable with matrix evidence rather than surprising; you can recruit senior testers because the path is legible; strong ICs like Dan advance and stay *without* being forced into management; and pay and title decisions stop generating grievances because they trace to demonstrated competencies everyone can see.$L$, $L$Design Northstar's split point concretely for its two most important people, then sanity-check it against reality. Below senior, one shared track (Junior → QA Engineer → Senior QA) keeps things simple at this scale. At senior it forks. Sofia, already doing informal leadership, is offered the **management track** — QA Manager / Head of QA — deliberately and with development into it (Lesson 5), so she chooses leadership rather than drifting into it unrecognised, and her pay finally matches the role she is already performing. Dan is offered the **technical track** — Principal / Staff QA focused on test architecture and infrastructure — so he can grow *up* from lone maintainer without being pushed into managing people, which he neither wants nor is ready for; his promotion evidence is the matrix showing him at 3–4 on CI/automation *and* newly demonstrating the org-wide influence and the second-person mentoring the level requires. Then the reality check that makes it honest: Northstar is 55 people, so there is perhaps one Principal and one Head-of-QA slot — you say that out loud, you do not invent a second senior slot to keep a third person happy, and you use scope and specialism growth for those without a vertical slot yet. That combination — a real dual fork *plus* candid headroom — is what separates a credible ladder from an HR poster, and it is exactly the honesty Priya will respect over a glossy nine-level framework the company cannot fill.$L$, $L$- Building a single ladder so the only route to advancement and higher pay is management, pushing good ICs out of testing or out of the door
- Describing levels by years of service or tool lists instead of demonstrated competencies tied to the skills matrix
- Inflating titles to retain people cheaply, which devalues every level and stores up a pay-and-credibility problem
- Promising vertical progression a small organisation cannot deliver, instead of being honest about headroom and using lateral growth$L$, $L$Write your level descriptions in the exact competency language of your skills matrix. When the same words describe both "how we assess you" and "what the next level requires", a promotion case writes itself from evidence, promotion decisions stop feeling arbitrary, and the framework becomes a live instrument instead of a document nobody consults.$L$, $L$Design a QA progression framework for Northstar: define the levels from junior to lead/principal in competency-and-scope terms (reusing your matrix competencies), show where and how the dual technical/management ladder splits, and write one honest paragraph on the headroom reality of a 55-person company and how you would handle progression when vertical slots are scarce. Map at least Sofia and Dan onto the framework with the evidence that would justify their next move.$L$, $L$In organisations you have worked in, was becoming a manager the only real way to advance and earn more? What did that cost those organisations in great individual contributors — and would a genuine dual ladder have changed it, or was the "dual" track a fiction everyone saw through?$L$, $L$An organisation introduces a dual technical/management ladder, but the top of the individual-contributor track pays noticeably less and carries less influence than the equivalent management level. Why does this fail to solve the problem it was meant to, and what happens as a result? (Answer: if the IC track pays and ranks below the management track it is not genuinely dual — everyone can see the real route to money and influence is still management, so strong individual contributors are still pushed toward management they may be unsuited to, or they leave; the framework fails because the tracks must be genuinely comparable in pay and status for the choice to be real.)$L$, array[$L$I can design competency-based levels with a genuine dual technical/management ladder for a mid-sized organisation$L$, $L$I can tie the progression framework to the skills matrix and development plans so it operates rather than decorates$L$, $L$I can make the honest trade-offs — headroom, level inflation, comparable dual-track pay — explicit to executives$L$]
